---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Prompt = `WRITTEN RESPONSE 2(c)

  You are given the procedure  isEqual(value1, value2),
  which returns true if the two values are equal and
  false otherwise.

  Using the list from your Personalized Project Reference,
  explain in detailed steps an algorithm that counts the
  number of elements equal to a certain value.

  Your explanation must be detailed enough for someone
  else to write the program code.`;

const Given = `PROCEDURE isEqual(value1, value2)
{
  <this is given to you. You do not write it.>
}

  isEqual(4, 4)        returns true
  isEqual("a", "b")    returns false`;

const Checklist = `The four things the algorithm MUST do:

  1  go through EVERY element of the list
  2  call isEqual on each element
  3  keep a running count
  4  produce the count -- store it, return it, or display it

Miss any one and the point is gone.`;

const Model = `colors ← ["red", "blue", "red", "green", "red"]

PROCEDURE countMatching(aList, target)
{
  count ← 0
  i ← 1

  REPEAT UNTIL(i > LENGTH(aList))
  {
    IF(isEqual(aList[i], target))
    {
      count ← count + 1
    }

    i ← i + 1
  }

  RETURN(count)
}`;

const BadOne = `count ← 0
i ← 1

REPEAT UNTIL(isEqual(aList[i], target))
{
  i ← i + 1
}

RETURN(i)`;

const BadTwo = `Go through the list and count how many
match the value, then return the count.`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Written Response Rehearsal: The Algorithm</h1>

        <h4 class="text-sn-theme">
          Four problems &middot; on paper &middot; this is Written Response 2(c)
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>Objective</h2>

          <Code code={Prompt} frame="code" lang="txt" title="The Real Prompt" showLineNumbers={false} class="my-3" />

          <p>
            <b>This is the most predictable question on the exam.</b> It is the same
            algorithm every year, over whatever list you happen to have put in your
            Project Reference. <b class="text-sn-theme">You can walk in already
            knowing how to answer it.</b>
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>You may write it in code, in exam notation, as numbered steps, or as
            a paragraph in English.</b> All four are explicitly allowed. What is not
            allowed is vagueness.
          </p>

          <Code code={Checklist} frame="code" lang="txt" title="What Earns the Point" showLineNumbers={false} class="my-3" />

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            <b>Paper only.</b>
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="problems">
          <h2>The Problems</h2>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Problem 1 &mdash; What You Are Given</h3>

          <Code code={Given} frame="code" lang="txt" title="isEqual" showLineNumbers={false} class="mb-3" />

          <ol>
            <li>The exam gives you <code>isEqual</code>. <b>Why does it bother?</b> You could write <code>aList[i] = target</code> instead.</li>
            <li>What would <code>isEqual</code> have to do that <code>=</code> might not, if the list held something more complicated than numbers?</li>
          </ol>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Problem 2 &mdash; Read a Model</h3>

          <Code code={Model} frame="code" lang="txt" title="countMatching" showLineNumbers={false} class="mb-3" />

          <ol>
            <li><b>Point at each of the four required things</b> in the checklist above. Write the line number of each.</li>
            <li>What does <code>countMatching(colors, &quot;red&quot;)</code> return?</li>
            <li>What does <code>countMatching(colors, &quot;yellow&quot;)</code> return? <b>Is that a failure?</b></li>
          </ol>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Problem 3 &mdash; Two Answers That Score Zero</h3>

          <p><b>Both of these were handed in by someone who understood the problem.</b> Neither earns the point.</p>

          <h5 class="mt-4">Attempt A</h5>

          <Code code={BadOne} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Attempt A" showLineNumbers={false} class="mb-3" />

          <ol>
            <li><b>Name every one of the four requirements this misses.</b></li>
            <li>What does it actually compute?</li>
            <li>Describe an input that makes it fail badly, not just return the wrong number.</li>
          </ol>

          <h5 class="mt-4">Attempt B</h5>

          <Code code={BadTwo} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Attempt B" showLineNumbers={false} class="mb-3" />

          <ol>
            <li>
              Everything in it is <b>true</b>. Why does it still score nothing?
            </li>
            <li>
              The prompt says <i>detailed enough for someone else to write the
              program code</i>. <b>Rewrite Attempt B so it passes that test</b>,
              staying in English.
            </li>
          </ol>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Problem 4 &mdash; Your Own</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Use the list from your own program.</b> Write the full Written
            Response 2(c) answer: name your list, pick a value, and give the
            algorithm.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then check it against the four-item checklist yourself.
            <b> All four, or it is not finished.</b>
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Keep this answer.</b> The version you write today
            is very close to the version you will write in May &mdash; only the list
            name changes.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            All four problems, plus your own Written Response 2(c) checked against
            the four-item checklist.
          </p>

          <p>Worth <b>5 points</b>, graded on completion.</p>
        </section>

        <h1 class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash">
          Commence Challenge
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
